        "Africa has never had so many tools for moving money within the continent. The Pan-African Payment and Settlement System (PAPSS) reports, according to figures presented in Lagos in September and reported by [Techeconomy](https://techeconomy.ng/papss-targets-accelerated-adoption-transaction-growth-as-network-expands-across-africa), that transaction volumes rose by about 1,000% and values by about 120% between comparable periods of 2025 and 2026, with more than 30 countries, 24 central banks and over 200 commercial banks and payment providers connected. The African Exchanges Linkage Project (AELP) now connects eleven stock exchanges. On 7 October, in Mauritius, the African Union launched its own rating agency, the Africa Credit Rating Agency (AfCRA). The institutions these tools are meant for, however, make limited use of them.",
        "On 3 November 2026 in Luanda, the Africa Financial Summit (AFIS) presented the sixth edition of its barometer of the African financial sector, produced with Deloitte and whose main results were published by [Sikafinance](https://www.sikafinance.com/marches/afis-2026-le-6e-barometre-du-secteur-financier-africain-dresse-un-bilan-contraste-des-avancees-de-lintegration-regionale_65496). The executives of banks, insurers, fintechs and capital-market firms who answer it remain upbeat: their average score for the three-year outlook stays at 8 out of 10. Their verdict on regional integration is far harsher. Only 7% see tangible progress in the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA), 69% find it too slow and 24% consider it stalled. Only 11% of the institutions surveyed are connected to PAPSS, and 45% have no plans to connect.",
