        "Rapid volume growth and a small share of connected institutions go hand in hand. A 1,000% rise measured from a very low base says nothing about PAPSS's share of intra-African payments, which neither the system nor the central banks publish. It points instead to a network at the very start of its adoption curve. The American economists Michael Katz and Carl Shapiro, professors at the University of California, Berkeley, showed in a 1985 article that such a network is subject to network externalities: the benefit each participant derives from it rises with the number of other participants[^1]. For a bank in Abidjan, joining PAPSS only makes sense if its clients trade with partners whose banks are also connected. As long as most counterparties stay outside, the bank keeps using its correspondent in Paris or New York, which in turn keeps the other banks outside.",
        "The same year, the economists Joseph Farrell and Garth Saloner, of Berkeley and Stanford, gave this equilibrium a name: excess inertia[^2]. When everyone waits for others to adopt a new standard, a more efficient technology can go unused for a long time without anyone making a mistake. The classic way out is for an actor able to bring a large group of users on board at once to step in. In Africa, that role falls to central banks. The BEAC played it by joining PAPSS on 9 July 2026, aiming to connect institutions in the six CEMAC countries, home to more than 72 million people, by the end of the year, according to [Launch Base Africa](https://launchbaseafrica.com/2026/07/09/papss-inches-closer-to-continent-wide-coverage-as-central-african-central-bank-joins/). The BCEAO chose a more cautious path: it will observe a six-month pilot with more than 80 WAEMU banks to check that PAPSS complies with the Union's regulations before making any formal commitment, a plan announced on 21 July and reported by [Ecofin Agency](https://www.ecofinagency.com/news-finances/2107-57583-bceao-prepares-papss-pilot-with-more-than-80-west-african-banks).",
