          "“Africa+1” summits have become an ordinary diplomatic format: about thirty between 1973 and 2008, and thirty-one since, according to the [Africa Policy Research Institute](https://afripoli.org/africa1-summits-a-closer-look-at-the-geometry-of-global-diplomacy). They converge in form, with a three-year cycle, co-chairmanship with the AU, business forums and action plans. They diverge in substance. In Beijing in 2024, China announced about $50.7 billion over three years, mostly in credit lines, and since May 2026 it has applied zero tariffs to imports from 53 African countries ([Brookings](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/2024-focac-beijing-summit-a-new-chapter/)). Japan is betting on private-sector finance alongside the African Development Bank, Korea on aid and export credit, and the European Union on the €150 billion Global Gateway package for Africa. The United States has turned away from large summits in favour of smaller formats and has extended AGOA until the end of 2028.",
