          "Both rules were already tested, in the opposite direction, during the 2025-2026 season. Abidjan opened that season at a record 2,800 CFA francs/kg, then cut the price to 1,200 CFA francs in March, when world prices, which had topped $11,000 per tonne in late 2024, halved and unsold stocks piled up; the regulator only finished buying back some 100,000 tonnes of beans left without a buyer in August. Accra followed in February 2026, lowering its price from 58,000 to 41,392 cedis per tonne because buyers were refusing Ghanaian beans at the set price, according to [Reuters](https://www.foodbusinessmea.com/ghana-cocobod-seeks-us1-4b-domestic-funding-for-2026-27-cocoa-purchases/). Licensed buyers were still claiming about 4 billion cedis from Cocobod for the previous season. On the way down, then, the two boards absorbed the same shock and corrected their prices within weeks of each other; it was the recovery that pulled their paths apart.",
