          "On June 16 in Abidjan, Alassane Ouattara and John Dramani Mahama signed a declaration committing to harmonize farmgate prices, premiums and season calendars, and to open the Côte d'Ivoire-Ghana Cocoa Initiative to other producers ([CIGCI](https://www.cighci.org/presidents-of-cote-divoire-and-ghana-agree-to-harmonise-cocoa-farm-gate-pricing-policies/)). A month later, Nigeria, Ghana, Côte d'Ivoire and Cameroon adopted a declaration in Abuja aimed at ending raw bean exports ([Arise News](https://www.arise.tv/nigeria-ghana-cote-divoire-cameroon-sign-abuja-declaration-to-end-raw-cocoa-exports/)). The four signatories, which claim about 75% of world output, also undertook to negotiate with buyers as a bloc and to defend a common position on the European regulation, seeking recognition of their national traceability systems. A joint Ivorian-Ghanaian technical group met in Accra on July 28 and 29. Yet three months after Abidjan, the world's two largest producers are paying their farmers prices that differ by 75%.",
          "The American economist George Stigler, winner of the 1982 Nobel Prize in economics, explained in “A Theory of Oligopoly” (1964) why agreements among sellers come apart: they hold only if each party can observe the other's conduct and if deviating from the rule carries a cost, failing which each member is tempted to break it quietly in order to sell more[^5]. The June declaration sets an objective without a common formula. On one side, a price indexed to at least 70% of FOB; on the other, a price anchored in forward sales and in 60% of the CIF price, with a floor of 50% in periods of high volatility. Each country can apply its own rule to the letter and end up with a price far removed from its neighbour's, without ever formally breaching the agreement. The problem, then, is not cheating in Stigler's sense but the absence of a rule from which deviation would be observable: without a shared benchmark, neither country can accuse the other of breaking it.",
          "The geography of the Abuja declaration makes the question more pressing. The four signatories export through six major ports, from San Pedro to Douala, and their pricing policies range from Ghanaian indexation to the liberalized markets of Nigeria and Cameroon, where the farmgate price follows world prices without a stabilization board. A collective commitment to local processing presupposes that each country keeps its beans; it becomes fragile if price gaps between neighbours shift supply toward whichever pays most, even when that country has less grinding capacity. The monitoring council provided for under Nigeria's value-addition agreement will publish annual reports, but no regional instrument yet links farmgate prices to processing targets.",
          "Yet the two countries do know how to apply a common rule when it is simple and public: the Living Income Differential of $400 per tonne has applied to both origins since the 2020-2021 season, and both boards have published their origin differentials every month since 2022. Credible harmonization of the farmgate price would require the same kind of instrument: a common share of the FOB price, a single reference window for forward sales, a simultaneous mid-season review and a financing mechanism able to absorb the gap between contracted prices and spot prices. Otherwise the country that sells early will remain exposed, every time prices recover, to what Bates described as producers' ordinary response to implicit taxation: exit, which here takes the form of supply shifting to the neighbour."
