        "heading": "A text about method, a message of recognition",
        "paragraphs": [
          "The communiqué makes no mention of free movement, trade, corridors or security. It refers to “areas of common interest” without listing them and sets neither a mandate nor a timetable. The silence serves a purpose: a text that lays down a method and leaves the substance to technical experts allows each side to protect its red lines in front of its domestic audience. AES governments have built their legitimacy on a narrative of regained sovereignty and cannot be seen to be negotiating a return; ECOWAS leaders must avoid giving the impression that exit comes cheap. A vague mandate leaves each side free to present the meeting as a victory, and defers the costly trade-offs to a moment when concrete results can justify them.",
          "The form, by contrast, is political. Equal co-chairmanship and the “entity-to-entity” negotiation demanded by the AES amount to de facto recognition of the Confederation as a counterpart. ECOWAS has been down this road before with Mauritania, which left in 2000: it first pressed Nouakchott to return, then concluded an association agreement with it in 2017. Seventeen years separate those two dates; this time, the West African organization took twenty-one months to accept dealing with a rival organization rather than with three states taken one by one. The shorter interval reflects the density of the trade at stake, far greater than anything that bound Nouakchott to the rest of the region, and the cost of the 2022 and 2023 sanctions, which has already been observed.",
          "The choice of Dakar is no accident. In July 2024, ECOWAS appointed Senegalese President Bassirou Diomaye Faye, together with Togolese President Faure Gnassingbé, to lead mediation with the three Sahelian countries, and Senegal is one of the few coastal states to maintain steady relations with Bamako, its largest customer. By entrusting the co-chairmanship to Senegalese and Burkinabè ministers rather than to the presidents of the two commissions, the parties opted for an intergovernmental format, which commits states more directly than secretariats do. That is a sign of seriousness, but also a source of fragility: the dialogue will depend on the constancy of a handful of capitals, above all Nigeria, which carries the most weight within ECOWAS and has the most strained relations with Niamey."
        ]
