          "Transport costs are the other component. The economists Nuno Limão, of the University of Maryland, and Anthony Venables, a British economist who long taught at the University of Oxford, showed in 2001, in a study published in the World Bank Economic Review, that the quality of infrastructure in the exporting, importing and transit countries explains much of the variation in transport costs, and that for a landlocked country the quality of its neighbour's roads matters almost as much as its own[^3]. Africa has sixteen landlocked countries. For them, a zero tariff negotiated at continental level remains theoretical if the truck waits several days at a border or the corridor runs through an insecure zone. In its [African Economic Outlook 2018](https://icafrica.org/en/knowledge-hub/article/african-economic-outlook-2018-351), the African Development Bank put the continent's infrastructure needs at $130 billion to $170 billion a year, with a financing gap of $68 billion to $108 billion.",
